# Comments

A comment may only state something the code cannot say: an external-system fact,
a rationale for a choice that would otherwise look wrong, a workaround, or a
perf/safety constraint. If it fails that test, do not write it.

## Form

- Every comment in TS/TSX is a JSDoc block. No bare `//` comments.
- One line ideally, two max: `/** Summary sentence. */`
- If it needs more than two lines, move the explanation to a README and leave `@see`.
- CSS uses a one-line `/* … */` with the same content test.

## Allowed JSDoc properties

- Summary line — one sentence; this is the whole comment in most cases
- `@remarks` — a second line of rationale when the summary alone isn't enough (rare)
- `@todo` — actionable follow-ups
- `@see` — cross-reference to the README, ADR, or symbol holding the longer explanation
- `@deprecated` — with the replacement named
- Never `@param` / `@returns` — TypeScript signatures already carry that

## Content rules

- **Why, never what.** If the code already says it, no comment.
- **No history.** Never reference how the code used to work or why it changed. Git holds that.
- **No asides.** No parentheticals, hedges, hypotheticals ("raise only if…"), or best-practice sermons.
- **No editorializing.** State the constraint, not the drama.
- **File headers:** a single one-line JSDoc block, only when the module's purpose isn't obvious from its name.
- **Exempt:** tool directives (`oxlint-disable`, `ts-expect-error`), license headers, generated files.

## Example

```ts
// Bad (5 lines of narration, asides, editorializing):
// Auto-stop when the window is hidden/occluded (tray, another Space, minimized).
// Background GPU/CPU inference is the worst kind of heat — the machine warms up
// for work the user can't even see — so we gate scheduling on visibility.
// (Best practice: only do heavy on-device inference while the surface is
// actually on screen.)

// Good:
/** Inference runs on the UI renderer; pause while hidden so it never burns CPU/GPU invisibly. */
```
